window.PORTAL={"day": 9, "title": "PDA + PPHN + Neonatal Hemodynamics", "docTitle": "DAY 9 — NEET-SS + ICP PAEDIATRICS 2026 PDA + PPHN + NEONATAL HEMODYNAMICS", "subtitle": ["Enhanced V3 FINAL TUTOR-REVIEWED EDITION • Nelson 22nd conceptual framework • current-guideline overlay • 75 base MCQs + 4 extra notebook retests • balanced irregular answer positions"], "source": "source/Day_9_NEET_SS_ICP_Paediatrics_FINAL_Tutor_Reviewed.docx", "pages": [{"id": "overview", "title": "Overview", "group": "Start", "kind": "overview", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Enhanced V3 FINAL TUTOR-REVIEWED EDITION • Nelson 22nd conceptual framework • current-guideline overlay • 75 base MCQs + 4 extra notebook retests • balanced irregular answer positions</strong>"}]}, {"title": "Day 9 Objectives", "h1": "Day 9 Objectives", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Distinguish ductal patency from a hemodynamically consequential PDA using integrated clinical and echocardiographic evidence.", "Reason from PVR/SVR relationships, shunt direction and ventricular loading in PPHN rather than relying on saturation alone.", "Interpret pre/postductal SpO₂, ductal flow, TR-derived RV pressure, septal configuration, LA/LV loading and systemic diastolic steal.", "Sequence stabilization, lung recruitment, perfusion support, selective pulmonary vasodilation and rescue escalation safely.", "Recognize when the ductus is harmful, neutral or essential before attempting closure."]}]}]}, {"id": "part-1", "title": "Guideline / Exam Framework", "eyebrow": "", "group": "Study Notes", "kind": "notes", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Nelson 22nd is the primary conceptual reference. Current PDA practice is deliberately selective: echocardiography is essential for confirmation and hemodynamic assessment, while routine early closure of every preterm PDA has not shown long-term outcome benefit. Thresholds such as ductal diameter or LA:Ao are exam anchors, not stand-alone mandates. PPHN management is physiology-led: optimize lung recruitment and gas exchange, correct acidosis, support systemic/RV perfusion, use selective pulmonary vasodilation in appropriate infants, and exclude structural congenital heart disease."}, {"t": "p", "html": "India overlay: bedside practice should follow local NICU/NNF protocols for drug availability, dosing, referral and advanced rescue pathways. When local protocol differs from an international source, the question stem must specify the framework."}]}]}, {"id": "part-2", "title": "Core Notes", "eyebrow": "Part I", "group": "Study Notes", "kind": "notes", "sections": [{"title": "1. Transitional circulation: pressure-flow map", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "At birth, lung aeration, rising oxygen tension and removal of the low-resistance placental circuit drive PVR down and SVR up. The direction of flow across the PDA and PFO therefore depends on the relative pulmonary and systemic pressures. A patent ductus is an anatomic conduit; its clinical meaning comes from direction, magnitude, duration, ventricular response and organ perfusion."}]}, {"title": "2. PDA: from open duct to consequential shunt", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "As PVR falls, a preterm PDA usually shunts left-to-right. Consequences of a large shunt are pulmonary overcirculation (edema, rising oxygen/ventilator need, pulmonary hemorrhage risk, LA/LV volume loading) and systemic diastolic steal (low diastolic pressure, oliguria, altered gut/cerebral perfusion). Clinical signs are supportive but imperfect. Echo integrates duct size, flow pattern, LA/LV loading and systemic arterial diastolic flow. Historical exam anchors include ductal diameter around ≥1.5 mm and LA:Ao ≥1.5, but neither should be used alone as a treatment mandate."}]}, {"title": "3. Modern PDA treatment principle", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Do not treat ductal patency alone. Routine prophylactic/very early closure has not consistently improved death, BPD or neurodevelopment. Stable infants without a consequential shunt can be observed with serial reassessment. When a persistent PDA is plausibly contributing to respiratory or systemic compromise, pharmacologic closure may be considered after checking contraindications and local protocol. Ibuprofen/indomethacin are COX inhibitors; acetaminophen is used in some pathways but evidence and safety considerations differ. Persistent symptomatic PDA after medical therapy may prompt transcatheter or surgical discussion. Post-closure/ligation hemodynamic deterioration is an important complication."}]}, {"title": "4. PPHN: core physiology", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "PPHN is failure of the normal postnatal fall in PVR, producing high RV afterload and right-to-left/bidirectional shunting at the PDA and/or PFO. Causes include parenchymal lung disease (MAS, pneumonia/sepsis, RDS), maladaptation, pulmonary hypoplasia and developmental vascular abnormalities. Hypoxemia, acidosis, hypercarbia, pain/agitation, atelectasis and overdistension can all raise PVR."}]}, {"title": "5. PPHN bedside and echo assessment", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Measure preductal saturation at the right hand and postductal saturation at a foot. A persistent pre &gt; post gradient supports ductal right-to-left shunting, but an absent gradient does not exclude PPHN. Echo is essential to exclude structural CHD and assess shunt direction, TR-derived RV pressure estimate, septal flattening, RV/LV function and filling. A D-shaped LV reflects RV pressure loading."}]}, {"title": "6. PPHN management sequence", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Stabilize temperature, glucose and perfusion; minimize agitation. Treat the underlying lung disease and recruit lung volume without overdistension. Correct important hypoxemia, hypercarbia and acidosis. Support systemic and RV perfusion using physiology-guided vasoactive therapy. In an eligible term/near-term infant with persistent hypoxemic respiratory failure after lung optimization, inhaled nitric oxide is a selective pulmonary vasodilator. Avoid abrupt iNO withdrawal because rebound PH can occur. Severe refractory disease requires early discussion with an ECMO-capable center when eligibility permits."}]}, {"title": "7. Oxygenation index", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "OI = FiO₂ × mean airway pressure × 100 / PaO₂. It quantifies the intensity of ventilatory support relative to arterial oxygenation. Trends are more useful than one isolated value. Traditional exam frameworks often use persistent OI around 15–25 as severe disease prompting escalation/iNO consideration in eligible infants and OI around 40 despite optimal therapy as a classic ECMO-severity anchor; actual ECMO decisions are center-, gestation-, weight- and contraindication-dependent."}]}, {"title": "8. Hemodynamic traps", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Do not close a ductus until its role is understood: in duct-dependent systemic or pulmonary congenital heart disease, patency can be lifesaving. In severe PH, a right-to-left ductus can act as a pressure pop-off for the RV. Conversely, a large left-to-right PDA can steal systemic diastolic flow. 'Open duct' and 'bad duct' are not synonyms."}]}, {"title": "9. PPHN pharmacology and practical options — exam anchors with protocol caveats", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Inhaled nitric oxide (iNO): after optimal lung recruitment/ventilation and echocardiographic confirmation when feasible, current neonatal guidance supports iNO for late-preterm/term infants with hypoxemic respiratory failure and PPHN physiology. A practical exam anchor is OI &gt;15–20 despite optimisation; many classic trials enrolled at higher OI. Start at 20 ppm in near-term/term infants, assess for a prompt response, and avoid abrupt discontinuation because rebound pulmonary hypertension can occur. Routine iNO for preterm respiratory failure is not recommended, although selected rescue scenarios are recognised in specialist guidance."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Alternative pulmonary vasodilators are protocol- and setting-dependent. Sildenafil and prostacyclin analogues are used in selected centres when iNO is unavailable or as adjuncts; systemic hypotension and ventricular physiology must be considered. Milrinone can support ventricular function and lower PVR but may worsen systemic hypotension, so loading doses are often avoided in unstable neonates. Bosentan and magnesium sulfate are not routine first-line neonatal PPHN therapies and should not be memorised as universal rescue steps."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Systemic support must be physiology-led: choose inotropy/vasopressor support according to RV function, LV function and systemic vascular tone rather than aiming for an arbitrary high blood pressure. Dobutamine/epinephrine may support low output; norepinephrine or vasopressin may be useful in vasodilatory shock; milrinone is an inodilator when blood pressure can tolerate it. Hydrocortisone may be considered for refractory hypotension according to NICU protocol."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Lung strategy: surfactant for parenchymal disease (MAS, RDS), HFOV where diffuse disease limits recruitment, and avoiding overdistension, hyperventilation or alkali infusion. Aim for a near-normal pH (about 7.30–7.45), avoid both hypoxemia and prolonged hyperoxia, and minimise handling with adequate sedation/analgesia. Treat sepsis and correct glucose, calcium and anemia."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Special settings: CDH (variable iNO response; check LV function), HIE with therapeutic hypothermia (cooling can raise PVR and slow the heart rate), and pulmonary hypoplasia."}, {"t": "p", "html": "ECMO eligibility is centre-specific and depends on gestation, weight, reversibility of disease, bleeding risk and major anomalies. OI ≈40 remains a classic exam severity anchor, but a rapidly rising OI despite optimized ventilation, iNO and hemodynamic support should trigger early discussion with an ECMO-capable centre rather than waiting for a single threshold."}]}, {"title": "10. PDA: drugs, contraindications and echo markers — exam anchors with protocol caveats", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Common PDA pharmacotherapy anchor: ibuprofen 10 mg/kg followed by 5 mg/kg at 24 and 48 hours is widely used. Acetaminophen/paracetamol (often 15 mg/kg every 6 hours in published regimens) is an alternative in selected infants, including when NSAIDs are unsuitable. Indomethacin dosing varies with postnatal age and protocol; use the unit-specific neonatal regimen rather than a single universal dose schedule."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Important NSAID cautions/contraindications include active bleeding, significant thrombocytopenia, clinically important renal dysfunction or oliguria, NEC/spontaneous intestinal perforation, and duct-dependent congenital heart disease. Fixed laboratory cut-offs are protocol/trial dependent and should not be presented as universal. Concomitant early indomethacin and systemic corticosteroid exposure has been associated with intestinal perforation risk in very preterm infants."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Evidence anchor: randomised trials comparing expectant management with early drug treatment (for example BeNeDuctus and Baby-OSCAR) have not shown benefit from early closure on major outcomes, supporting selective treatment."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Echo markers that can support a consequential PDA include ductal size indexed to infant size, flow pattern/velocity, LA/LV volume loading, increased pulmonary blood flow surrogates and absent/reversed systemic diastolic flow. Historical anchors such as duct diameter around &gt;1.5 mm or LA:Ao ≥1.5 are useful for examinations but are not stand-alone treatment mandates. BNP/NT-proBNP can support assessment but does not replace echocardiography."}]}, {"title": "11. PPHN echo refinements", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Septal flattening in systole indicates RV pressure overload, whereas flattening in diastole indicates RV volume overload."}, {"t": "p", "html": "TR-derived RV systolic pressure ≈ 4 × (TR velocity)² + estimated right atrial pressure. Compare with systemic blood pressure, and look at the direction of PDA and PFO shunts, RV function and LV filling."}, {"t": "p", "html": "A pre/postductal difference of about ≥5–10 percentage points supports ductal R→L shunting; an atrial-level R→L shunt can produce severe hypoxemia without a gradient."}]}, {"title": "EXAM–PRACTICE CALIBRATION", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "For this chapter, memorize the classic exam anchors (e.g., OI formula, iNO 20 ppm, LA:Ao around 1.5, duct diameter around 1.5 mm) but answer management questions using the specified framework. Current practice emphasizes composite physiology and trajectory; no single PDA echo number or PPHN OI threshold should be treated as an automatic intervention mandate."}]}]}, {"id": "numbers", "title": "Must-Know Numbers", "group": "Quick Reference", "kind": "notes", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Item</strong>", "<strong>Exam anchor</strong>", "<strong>Interpretation / caution</strong>"], "rows": [["Preductal site", "Right hand", "Postductal = either foot."], ["LA:Ao", "≈ ≥1.5", "Supports left-heart volume loading; not a stand-alone treatment threshold."], ["Duct diameter", "≈ ≥1.5 mm historical anchor", "Interpret with body size, flow pattern and clinical burden."], ["Systemic steal", "Absent/reversed diastolic flow", "Descending aorta / mesenteric / cerebral or renal arterial patterns can support runoff."], ["OI formula", "FiO₂ × MAP ×100 / PaO₂", "Use decimal FiO₂; keep units consistent."], ["PPHN differential saturation", "Preductal &gt; postductal", "Supports ductal R→L shunt; absent gradient does not exclude PPHN."], ["Pre/postductal SpO₂ gradient", "≈ ≥5–10 percentage points (exam anchor)", "Supports ductal R→L shunting; interpret with echo and repeat measurements."]]}]}]}, {"id": "tables", "title": "Tables & Algorithms", "group": "Quick Reference", "kind": "notes", "sections": [{"title": "Algorithm 1 — Suspected hsPDA", "h1": "Part II — Decision Algorithms", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": true, "items": ["Identify clinical burden: respiratory escalation, pulmonary edema, low diastolic BP, oliguria/perfusion concern.", "Confirm PDA and direction by echo.", "Assess shunt magnitude: ductal flow/size + LA/LV loading + systemic diastolic flow.", "Exclude duct-dependent structural physiology and major contraindications.", "If stable/nonconsequential → observe and reassess.", "If persistent consequential shunt → consider protocol-based pharmacologic closure.", "If persistent after therapy with ongoing burden → multidisciplinary transcatheter/surgical evaluation."]}]}, {"title": "Algorithm 2 — Suspected PPHN", "h1": "", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": true, "items": ["Stabilize ABCs, temperature, glucose; obtain pre/postductal SpO₂ and blood gas.", "Treat lung disease; recruit lung without overdistension; correct acidosis/hypercarbia/hypoxemia.", "Echo urgently: exclude CHD; define PDA/PFO shunt, RV pressure/function and LV function.", "Support systemic/RV perfusion according to physiology.", "Persistent hypoxemic failure in eligible infant → selective pulmonary vasodilation (iNO).", "Reassess response and avoid abrupt withdrawal.", "Refractory severe disease → early ECMO-capable-center discussion when appropriate."]}]}]}, {"id": "data", "title": "Data Interpretation", "group": "Practice", "kind": "data", "sections": [{"title": "Visual Station 1 — PDA pressure-flow schematic", "blocks": [{"t": "img", "src": "img/image4_q.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image4.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Purpose-built educational schematic; not a patient image/recording."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Question: What two major physiologic consequences are shown?"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Interpretation: </strong>Pulmonary overcirculation and systemic diastolic steal."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Decisive visual/data features: </strong>L→R ductal runoff sends excess flow to lungs while reducing effective systemic diastolic perfusion."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Closest distractor / discriminator: </strong>PPHN with R→L ductal flow; direction and pressure context distinguish it."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Next management step: </strong>Confirm shunt magnitude and organ effects by echo/clinical assessment."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Exam trap: </strong>Do not call every open duct hemodynamically significant."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trace-back: </strong>Core notes §2."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 2 — Differential saturation", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Purpose-built educational schematic; not a patient image/recording."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Question: Interpret the right-hand versus foot saturation pattern."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/gen_diffsat_96_84.png", "alt": "", "caption": "Generated radiograph-style teaching schematic — not a patient image."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image5.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Interpretation: </strong>A large preductal &gt; postductal difference supports right-to-left ductal shunting."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Decisive visual/data features: </strong>Right hand samples preductal blood; the foot receives descending-aortic blood influenced by ductal shunt."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Closest distractor / discriminator: </strong>Cyanotic CHD can also produce differential patterns; echo is required."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Next management step: </strong>Urgent echo plus optimization of oxygenation/ventilation/perfusion."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Exam trap: </strong>Absence of a gradient does not exclude PPHN."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trace-back: </strong>Core notes §5."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 3 — Ductal Doppler: restrictive vs unrestrictive pattern", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Purpose-built educational schematic; not a patient image/recording."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Question: What should be extracted from ductal Doppler rather than simply labeling 'PDA present'?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/gen_doppler_ab.png", "alt": "", "caption": "Generated radiograph-style teaching schematic — not a patient image."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image6.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Interpretation: Interpret direction, velocity and temporal pattern together. Higher-velocity continuous L→R flow suggests a restrictive duct; lower-velocity pulsatile L→R flow suggests a smaller pressure gradient/unrestrictive duct.</strong>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Decisive visual/data features: The spectral pattern reflects the transductal pressure gradient; it does not by itself quantify total shunt volume.</strong>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Closest distractor / discriminator: Duct diameter alone is insufficient; pair size with flow pattern, chamber loading and systemic diastolic flow.</strong>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Next management step: </strong>Integrate Doppler with chamber loading and systemic flow."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Exam trap: </strong>Do not infer hemodynamic significance from patency alone."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trace-back: </strong>Core notes §2."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 4 — Echo data matrix", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Duct</strong>", "<strong>2.1 mm L→R</strong>"], "rows": [["LA:Ao", "1.8"], ["Descending aorta", "Diastolic reversal"], ["Clinical", "Rising ventilation + oliguria"]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Question: </strong>Which finding most directly demonstrates systemic steal?"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Interpretation: </strong>Descending-aortic diastolic reversal."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Decisive visual/data features: </strong>It is direct evidence of runoff during diastole."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Closest distractor / discriminator: </strong>LA:Ao 1.8 indicates pulmonary overcirculation/left-heart load, not systemic steal itself."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Next management step: </strong>Integrate with clinical burden before targeted closure."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Exam trap: </strong>One echo threshold should not be used alone."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trace-back: </strong>Core notes §2."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 5 — PPHN pressure pattern", "blocks": [{"t": "img", "src": "img/image7.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>TR-derived RV pressure</strong>", "<strong>≈ systemic/suprasystemic</strong>"], "rows": [["Septum", "Flattened / D-shaped LV"], ["PDA", "Bidirectional → R→L"], ["LV function", "Preserved"]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Question: </strong>What unifies these findings?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/gen_septum.png", "alt": "", "caption": "Generated radiograph-style teaching schematic — not a patient image."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image9.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Interpretation: </strong>High PVR with RV pressure overload consistent with PPHN physiology."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Decisive visual/data features: </strong>Multiple independent echo clues point to elevated pulmonary pressure."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Closest distractor / discriminator: </strong>Large L→R PDA would instead favor pulmonary overcirculation and LA/LV loading."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Next management step: </strong>Treat triggers of high PVR and support perfusion; consider iNO if eligible after lung optimization."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Exam trap: </strong>Echo defines physiology; 'PPHN' is not diagnosed from one saturation value."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trace-back: </strong>Core notes §4–6."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 6 — OI trend", "blocks": [{"t": "img", "src": "img/gen_oi_trend.png", "alt": "", "caption": "Generated radiograph-style teaching schematic — not a patient image."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image8.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Time</strong>", "<strong>0 h</strong>", "<strong>2 h</strong>", "<strong>4 h</strong>"], "rows": [["OI", "18", "27", "39"], ["iNO", "No", "Yes", "Yes"], ["Acidosis", "Corrected", "Corrected", "Corrected"]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Question: </strong>How should the trend be interpreted?"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Interpretation: </strong>Progressive severe hypoxemic respiratory failure despite escalation; activate rescue/ECMO-center pathway if eligible."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Decisive visual/data features: </strong>OI is worsening despite correction of reversible contributors and selective vasodilation."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Closest distractor / discriminator: </strong>A single lower early OI should not falsely reassure when the trajectory is rapidly worsening."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Next management step: </strong>Continue stabilization while urgently discussing advanced rescue."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Exam trap: </strong>Trend and eligibility matter; do not wait for catastrophic collapse."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trace-back: </strong>Core notes §7."}]}]}, {"id": "quizzes", "title": "Quizzes", "group": "Practice", "kind": "quizzes", "sections": []}, {"id": "revision", "title": "Rapid Revision", "group": "Revise", "kind": "revision", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Open duct ≠ hsPDA. Integrate clinical burden + ductal flow/size + LA/LV loading + systemic diastolic flow.", "Large L→R PDA: pulmonary overcirculation + systemic diastolic steal.", "Right hand = preductal; foot = postductal.", "Pre &gt; post SpO₂ supports ductal R→L shunt; absent gradient does not exclude PPHN.", "PPHN = high PVR → RV afterload + R→L/bidirectional PDA/PFO shunting.", "Echo in PPHN: exclude CHD; assess TR-derived RV pressure, septal shape, shunts, RV/LV function.", "Treat lung first: recruit without overdistension; correct hypoxemia, hypercarbia and acidosis.", "iNO is selective pulmonary vasodilation in appropriate eligible infants after lung optimization; wean gradually.", "OI = FiO₂ × MAP ×100 / PaO₂. Rising trend = worsening support burden.", "Do not close a ductus before excluding duct-dependent CHD or understanding whether it is serving as a pressure/pop-off pathway.", "Where iNO is unavailable: sildenafil, inhaled/IV prostacyclin; milrinone is an inodilator (watch hypotension); surfactant for parenchymal disease; avoid hyperventilation and alkali infusion.", "Closure drugs: ibuprofen 10-5-5 mg/kg; paracetamol is an option when NSAIDs are contraindicated; check platelets, creatinine, bleeding, NEC and early steroids first.", "Refer early for ECMO (persistent OI ≥25–30 in many centres); OI ≈40 is the classic anchor, not the starting line."]}]}]}, {"id": "recall", "title": "Active Recall", "group": "Revise", "kind": "recall", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": true, "items": ["Why can a large L→R PDA reduce systemic organ perfusion even when total cardiac output is not low?<br>Answer: Diastolic runoff through the ductus diverts effective systemic flow toward the pulmonary circuit.", "Name three echo domains used to judge PDA hemodynamic significance.<br>Answer: Duct size/flow pattern, LA/LV volume loading, and systemic diastolic-flow effects; integrate clinical burden.", "What is the standard preductal saturation site?<br>Answer: Right hand.", "Does an absent pre/postductal gradient exclude PPHN?<br>Answer: No. Shunting may be predominantly atrial or the gradient may be small despite PPHN.", "List four reversible factors that can raise PVR.<br>Answer: Hypoxemia, acidosis, hypercarbia, pain/agitation, atelectasis and overdistension are key examples.", "State the OI formula.<br>Answer: OI = FiO₂ × mean airway pressure × 100 / PaO₂.", "What is the classic physiology of a D-shaped LV in PPHN?<br>Answer: RV pressure overload flattens/bows the interventricular septum and can impair LV filling.", "Why can abrupt iNO discontinuation be dangerous?<br>Answer: Rebound pulmonary hypertension can occur.", "When can a patent ductus be beneficial rather than harmful?<br>Answer: Duct-dependent CHD and, in selected severe PH physiology, as an RV pressure pop-off/systemic-flow pathway.", "Visual recall: which Doppler/systemic-flow clue most directly supports ductal steal?<br>Answer: Absent or reversed diastolic flow in the descending aorta/systemic arteries."]}]}]}, {"id": "notebook", "title": "Error Notebook", "group": "Revise", "kind": "notebook", "sections": []}, {"id": "plan", "title": "Revision Plan", "group": "Revise", "kind": "plan", "sections": [{"title": "Error-Notebook Repair — Due Today", "h1": "Error-Notebook Repair — Due Today", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Concept</strong>", "<strong>Source</strong>", "<strong>Type</strong>", "<strong>Failure</strong>", "<strong>Status</strong>", "<strong>Today</strong>", "<strong>Next</strong>"], "rows": [["DOPE: category for a sudden desaturation in a ventilated infant", "Day 5 Q34", "S", "1×", "Active", "Fresh retest", "R2 ~12 Oct"], ["Jensen BPD grade defined by invasive ventilation", "Day 5 Q4", "K", "1×", "Active", "Fresh retest", "R2 ~11 Oct"], ["Jensen BPD grading by respiratory-support mode", "Day 5 Q53", "K", "1×", "Active", "Fresh retest", "R2 ~13 Oct"], ["G6PD neonatal hemolysis and the DAT result", "Day 6 Q60", "K", "1×", "Active", "Fresh retest", "R2 ~12 Oct"]]}]}, {"title": "Self-Assessment & Rag", "h1": "Self-Assessment & Rag", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Domain</strong>", "<strong>Score</strong>", "<strong>RAG / next action</strong>"], "rows": [["Base MCQs", "__/75", "GREEN ≥85%; AMBER 70–84%; RED &lt;70%"], ["Notebook Retests — Extra", "__/4", "Correct fresh retest → Improving; failure → increment xTime and prioritize R1/R2/R3"], ["Active Recall", "__/10", "Any missed volatile rule returns to next recall block"], ["Visual/Data stations", "__/6", "Revisit the discriminator, not just the diagnosis"]]}]}]}, {"id": "references", "title": "References", "group": "More", "kind": "notes", "sections": [{"title": "References / Current Guideline Anchors", "h1": "References / Current Guideline Anchors", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22nd ed. — neonatal cardiovascular transition, PDA and pulmonary hypertension conceptual framework."}, {"t": "p", "html": "American Academy of Pediatrics Clinical Report: Ambalavanan N, Aucott SW, Salavitabar A, et al. Patent Ductus Arteriosus in Preterm Infants. Pediatrics. 2025;155(5):e2025071425."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Current neonatal pulmonary-hypertension practice should be cross-checked against local NICU/NNF protocols for drug dosing, ECMO eligibility and referral pathways."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Abman SH, Hansmann G, Archer SL, et al. Pediatric Pulmonary Hypertension: Guidelines From the American Heart Association and American Thoracic Society. Circulation. 2015;132:2037–2099."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Jensen EA, Dysart K, Gantz MG, et al. The diagnosis of bronchopulmonary dysplasia in very preterm infants. Am J Respir Crit Care Med. 2019;200(6):751–759."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Hundscheid T, Onland W, Kooi EMW, et al. Expectant management or early ibuprofen for patent ductus arteriosus (BeNeDuctus). N Engl J Med. 2023;388:980–990."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Cole FS, Alleyne C, Barks JDE, et al. NIH Consensus Development Conference: inhaled nitric oxide therapy for premature infants. Pediatrics. 2011;127:363–369."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Mitra S, Altit G; Canadian Paediatric Society, Fetus and Newborn Committee. Inhaled nitric oxide use in newborns. Paediatr Child Health. 2023;28(2):119–122."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Current-practice calibration used in this edition: iNO initiation in late-preterm/term HRF is considered after optimized ventilation/oxygenation and echocardiographic assessment; OI &gt;15–20 is a commonly cited guidance range, while OI ≈40 remains a classic severe-disease/ECMO exam anchor."}]}]}], "quizSets": [{"k": 1, "from": 1, "to": 16, "n": 16}, {"k": 2, "from": 17, "to": 32, "n": 16}, {"k": 3, "from": 33, "to": 48, "n": 16}, {"k": 4, "from": 49, "to": 64, "n": 16}, {"k": 5, "from": 65, "to": 79, "n": 15}], "mcqCount": 79};
